      "id": 1,
      "text": "Bulls Head takes its name from an 18th‑century tavern that proudly displayed a bull’s‑head sign at Victory Boulevard and Richmond Avenue.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulls_Head%2C_Staten_Island",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "During the American Revolution, that same tavern became the British loyalists’ headquarters on Staten Island.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulls_Head%2C_Staten_Island",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The tavern burned down in 1871, ending an era when it served as a stagecoach stop linking Port Richmond, Richmondtown, and ferry landings.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityneighborhoods.nyc/bulls-head",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Bulls Head sits where Victory Boulevard, Richmond Avenue, and Willowbrook Road converge, bridging suburban homes with the reclaimed Freshkills Park.",
